From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dariusz Majnert <dariusz.majnert@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 18:00:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] runtime-joint-and-spindle-availability

Ported to v2.10.0-pre2. Differences from the patch on the old base:

Acquisition of an unhomed joint still homes it automatically and reports
AVAILABLE only after homing succeeds, but the homing now runs through the
queued home cycle upstream built for G28.2 instead of the patch's own
homing wait. After the hardware ACK, motion enters HOMING and waits for
task's home request. Task issues it as a queued EMC_JOINT_HOME (FREE dip,
EXEC_WAITING_FOR_HOMING, start timeout, completion from motion's
homing_active, restoration of the prior trajectory mode) and then waits
for the acquisition to complete. Motion accepts that one JOINT_HOME for
the acquiring joint, starts it from a stationary FREE joint, and publishes
AVAILABLE or a homing fault in the servo cycle in which homing ends.

Removed from the old patch: motion clearing coord/teleop itself and
restoring the saved motion state on completion (return_motion_state), and
motion's own homing start timeout. Abort, E-stop and Machine Off cancel
the acquisition homing as they cancel a G28.2 homing wait.

HAL pins use the pin API of v2.10.0-pre2.

The interpreter wait release on PLAN_SYNCH, which the patch added for
direct requests and stale waits, now skips an MDI line that still owns
the wait (mdi_execute_wait); on v2.10.0-pre2 the unconditional release
left such a line READING forever (seen in tests/tool-wear-abort after
E-stop). tests/interp/m98m99/12-M99-endless-main-program expectations
gain the availability configuration and reordered ABORT, as in the
other motion-logger tests.
